          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "The thyroid is a gland in the neck. Its main job is to make thyroid hormone, mainly a hormone called thyroxine, which the body partly converts into a more active form. Together these hormones set how fast most tissues in the body work, which is called the metabolic rate. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nIn hypothyroidism, the thyroid does not make enough thyroid hormone. The brain's pituitary gland responds by releasing more thyroid-stimulating hormone, so a rising level of that hormone is often the first sign on a test. Most cases come from the thyroid gland itself failing. A less common cause is a problem with the pituitary or hypothalamus. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nTesting usually starts with the thyroid-stimulating hormone level. If it is raised, a second test measures free thyroxine. Low free thyroxine is called overt hypothyroidism, and normal free thyroxine with a raised stimulating hormone is called subclinical hypothyroidism. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*, endocrinology guidance.)\n\nThe sources I searched did not explain the symptoms of hypothyroidism, its treatment, or how it is diagnosed beyond these tests. If you have symptoms or questions about your own thyroid, please discuss them with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
